// main.vix — the facet package, entire. The manifest is frontmatter
// (r[vixen.package.frontmatter]) and the code it names follows it in the same
// file. There is exactly one carrier: a separate package.styx does not exist,
// so no reader handles two forms and no precedence rule is needed.
//
// The seam is one sentence: above the fence NAMES things (fns, paths), below
// it COMPUTES things. Nothing above is evaluated. The fence is a quote-family
// block literal, and the quote family admits no holes at all
// (r[lang.literal.block]) — referencing a binding or a computed version
// inside it is not forbidden, it is unrepresentable. That is the structural
// answer to the failure flake.nix had to patch with a restricted evaluator.
//
// Spelling notes, learned from the real parser: a styx entry is at most two
// atoms (key + value), so every section is a MAP whose keys are the public
// names — there is no `command build { … }` three-atom form, and repeated
// keys are duplicate-key errors, not accumulation. A tag's payload must be
// adjacent (`@artifact{…}`, no space). Sequences use parentheses. One-field
// entries use attribute sugar (`build fn>facet::build` is parser[attr.atom]
// shorthand for `build { fn facet::build }` — same document, same decode).

manifest """
package {
  name facet
  version "0.1.0"
}

// `vx r facet build` — or `vx r build` from inside this package. A command is
// a bare fn ref: the fn is the whole recipe, the key is its public name. The
// fn's return type decides what `vx r` presents; its parameters declare its
// requirements; the manifest repeats neither.
//
// Note what is NOT here: any statement about where anything comes from. The
// manifest declares this package's SURFACE, not its provenance. Fetching is
// something the fns below do, as ordinary demands, whenever they like.
command {
  build fn>facet::build
  test fn>facet::test
}

// `vx build facet#bins` — demand facet::build, materialize the resulting Tree
// at ./result (vixen.delivery.result). Purity makes the second build a store
// hit; an artifact has no existence apart from its fn's value. Naming a fn
// here claims it returns Tree or Blob, and binding checks the claim before
// any effect runs — a record-returning fn fails at binding, not at delivery.
// The artifact key is committed API; the fn path is private and renameable.
artifact {
  bins fn>facet::build
}

// The executable this package exposes: a path into an artifact's value, plus
// the capability-package contracts. Which side of the seam a capability came
// from is invisible to consumers. Every enum value wears its @tag: a choice
// from a closed set is visibly a choice.
program {
  fx {
    source @artifact{
      name bins
      path "bin/fx"
    }
    protocol @exit-only
    target @neutral
  }
}
"""

// `workspace()` is a constant surface (binding::ConstantSurfaceDecl) the
// embedder declares over its own origin adapter — the promise recorded in
// vx.rs. It yields this package's own source tree, content-identified. It is
// the only door to "here": nothing else about the surrounding machine can
// reach this program, so the same demand on another box is the same demand.

// Build the workspace, hand back the tree of built binaries — bin/fx among
// them, which is all `program fx` in the manifest needs: a path into this
// fn's value. `artifact bins` claims this returns a Tree and the claim is
// checked against this signature at binding (vixen.delivery.result: only
// Tree and Blob render to a filesystem; anything else would be a guess).
//
// A manifest-named fn is a ROOT, with the same standing #[test] fns have
// today. Its capability parameters ARE its requirement declaration — the
// manifest never repeats them, and binding resolves them against what the
// executor offers, failing before any effect if nothing provides one.
fn build(rustc: Rustc, sh: Sh) -> Tree {
    // Whatever this package's build is. It fetches what it needs when it
    // needs it; the manifest above does not know and does not care.
    ...
}

// Commands may return anything `vx r` knows how to present; a Stream<Check>
// gets the same rendering the ratchet gives #[test] roots. A future typed
// argument surface (`vx r facet test --filter …`) is a command-grammar
// concern and lands on the manifest entry, not here.
fn test(rustc: Rustc, sh: Sh) -> Stream<Check> {
    ...
}
